<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teltow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teltow is a plateau region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> and Dahme rivers. In the 13th century the Ascanian margraves John I and Otto III won it in rivalry with the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and settled it with German colonists. The town of Teltow takes its name from the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teltow.jpg" alt="Teltow" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lienhard Schulz · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teltow3_Am_Marktplatz_und_Kirche_St_Andreas.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
